<!-- DRAFT v1 — corrections from the review applied. Rewrite freely in your own voice. -->
<section id="tldr" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="tldr">TL;DR</h2>
<ul>
<li>Over 2006–2026, <strong>three principal components explain 98.6%</strong> of the daily changes in the Brazilian PRE curve (3M to 10Y). Carol Alexander finds 99.2% for UK rates. Same structure: level, slope and curvature.</li>
<li>The Brazilian curve moves <strong>about five times more</strong> than the UK curve in the book (≈240 bp a year at the long end, against ≈50 bp), and still behaves like a highly correlated term structure. The book expected less from emerging markets.</li>
<li><strong>Covariance or correlation matters.</strong> With the covariance matrix, the level factor is weak at the front end — the short rates are anchored by the policy rate (Selic). With the correlation matrix, it is almost flat. Both shapes match the book’s UK results for the same choice.</li>
<li>The structure is stable over time. The exception is <strong>2009–10</strong>, when the front end and the long end stopped moving together and PC1’s share fell to about 75%.</li>
</ul>
