<section id="question" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="question">Question</h2>
<p>In <em>Market Risk Analysis</em> (Vol. II, ch.&nbsp;2), Carol Alexander runs a PCA on 50 UK spot rates, from 6 months to 25 years, using daily data from January 2005 to December 2007. On the correlation matrix, the first component — the level — explains 91.05% of the variation, the second — the slope — 6.85%, and the third — the curvature — 1.33%. Together, <strong>99.22%</strong>. Repeating the exercise on the covariance matrix of the short curve (1 to 60 months), the first three components explain 99.45%.</p>
<p>The book also warns that this works best for major currencies: in emerging markets, less liquid and less correlated, three components usually explain less.</p>
<p>So, <strong>does the same hold for the Brazilian curve?</strong> It is a good test: a high and volatile policy rate, frequent fiscal and political shocks, and short rates that ranged from about 2% to above 17% in the sample. PCA on the Brazilian curve goes back at least to Varga &amp; Valli (2001); this post revisits it with 20 years of public B3 data and open code.</p>
</section>
<section id="hypothesis-theory" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="hypothesis-theory">Hypothesis / Theory</h2>
<div class="callout callout-style-default callout-tip callout-titled" title="Primer: what PCA does to a yield curve">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center collapsed" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target=".callout-1-contents" aria-controls="callout-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Toggle callout">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-title-container flex-fill">
<span class="screen-reader-only">Tip</span>Primer: what PCA does to a yield curve
</div>
<div class="callout-btn-toggle d-inline-block border-0 py-1 ps-1 pe-0 float-end"><i class="callout-toggle"></i></div>
</div>
<div id="callout-1" class="callout-1-contents callout-collapse collapse">
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>Every day, nine points of the curve move — but not independently. When the 5Y rate rises, the 7Y almost always rises too. <strong>Principal Component Analysis</strong> finds the few independent <em>shapes</em> of movement that, combined, reproduce the daily changes of every tenor.</p>
<p>Each component comes with two things:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Loadings</strong> — one weight per tenor: the <em>shape</em> of the movement. If PC1 moves by one unit, the 10Y rate moves by its loading on PC1. All weights with the same sign is a shift of the whole curve (<em>level</em>); weights changing sign along the curve is a <em>slope</em>; ends against the middle is a <em>curvature</em>.</li>
<li><strong>Eigenvalue</strong> — the variance of that component, i.e.&nbsp;how much of the daily movement of the curve the shape accounts for. Divided by the sum of all eigenvalues, it gives the share of variance explained.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two practical choices matter. We use <strong>daily changes, not levels</strong>: rate levels trend for years and are not stationary, while daily changes are. And we pick a <strong>covariance</strong> matrix (each tenor keeps its own volatility) or a <strong>correlation</strong> matrix (every tenor standardized to the same volatility). As we will see, the choice changes the shapes.</p>
<p><strong>Go deeper:</strong> Alexander, <em>Market Risk Analysis</em>, Vol. II, ch.&nbsp;2; Litterman &amp; Scheinkman (1991), “Common Factors Affecting Bond Returns”, <em>Journal of Fixed Income</em>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Let <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5CSigma"> be the covariance matrix of the daily changes across the nine tenors. PCA decomposes it as</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5CSigma%20=%20W%20%5CLambda%20W%5E%5Ctop,%20%5Cqquad%20%5Ctext%7Bshare%7D_k%20=%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Clambda_k%7D%7B%5Csum_j%20%5Clambda_j%7D%0A"></p>
<p>where each column of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?W"> is an eigenvector — the <strong>loadings</strong> of one component — and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5CLambda"> is a diagonal matrix with the <strong>eigenvalues</strong> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda_1%20%5Cge%20%5Clambda_2%20%5Cge%20%5Cdots">: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda_k"> is the variance of component <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?k">, so <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctext%7Bshare%7D_k"> is the fraction of the total variance it explains.</p>
<p><strong>Hypothesis:</strong> three components explain more than 95% of the daily changes, as in the UK. Two Brazil-specific effects could break this: the higher volatility, and a front end tied to the Selic, which may move apart from the rest of the curve.</p>
</section>
<section id="data-method" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="data-method">Data &amp; Method</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Source:</strong> B3 daily reference-rate files (TS), curve PRE (DI × Pré).</li>
<li><strong>Constant maturities:</strong> 3M, 6M, 1Y, 2Y, 3Y, 4Y, 5Y, 7Y and 10Y. B3 publishes rates at fixed calendar-day vertices and at DI1 contract maturities, so each tenor is interpolated by <strong>flat forward</strong> (constant forward rate between vertices, 252 business-day basis), with no extrapolation.</li>
<li><strong>Sample:</strong> 2006-01-02 to 2026-10-27, 5,156 daily changes. It starts in 2006 because the 10Y tenor is only available from September 2005.</li>
<li><strong>PCA input:</strong> daily changes in basis points. Main results on the covariance matrix; the correlation matrix for the comparison with the book.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before running the PCA, it helps to look at what goes in. Brazilian rates are volatile, and much less so at the front end — the same shape as the UK curve in the book (Figure II.2.2), at a different scale.</p>
