<section id="results" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="results">Results</h2>
<p><strong>Three factors, as in the UK.</strong> PC1 explains 91.5%, PC2 5.8% and PC3 1.4% of the daily variance (Figure&nbsp;1), 98.6% in total — close to the 99.45% of the book’s UK covariance case, and well above the 95% the book would expect from an emerging market.</p>
<p><strong>The level factor lives at the long end.</strong> On the covariance matrix, PC1’s loading goes from 0.06 at 3M to about 0.40 from 3Y onwards: a one-unit move in PC1 moves the 10Y about six times more than the 3M. This is not a Brazilian peculiarity. Alexander finds the same for UK rates on the covariance matrix (Figure II.2.4): the first eigenvector “decreases markedly at the short end” and does not represent a parallel shift. The front end is low-volatility because it follows the policy rate, and a covariance PCA weights each tenor by its volatility.</p>
<p><strong>Covariance vs.&nbsp;correlation tells two different stories.</strong> Standardizing every tenor — using the correlation matrix — removes the volatility difference. PC1 becomes almost flat and explains 82.3%, while PC2 grows to 13.2% (Figure&nbsp;3). The correlation shapes are those of the book’s Figure II.2.3: a level slightly lower at the short end, a slope decreasing with maturity, and a curvature high at the short end, negative in the middle and rising again at the long end.</p>
