<p><strong>Do the components really mean level, slope and curvature?</strong> A quick check: correlate each component with a simple measure built by hand from the same daily changes (Table&nbsp;1). We use the 1Y, not the 3M, as the short leg: the 3M is tied to the Copom decisions and would mix monetary policy into the slope. The book notes the same for the UK, where the shortest rates are “more influenced by government policies”. Level and slope map cleanly onto PC1 and PC2. The hand-made curvature maps onto PC3 but also picks up part of PC1, because the level factor moves the belly (3Y) more than the average of the wings. The signs follow the book’s convention: a rise in PC2 lifts the front end and lowers the long end, so it moves against the 10Y − 1Y spread.</p>
