<section id="caveats" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="caveats">Caveats</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Covariance or correlation.</strong> The two give different shapes, as shown above. Covariance is the natural choice for risk and hedging, since it keeps the volatilities; correlation is the one to compare with the book’s main case.</li>
<li><strong>October 2026.</strong> The two largest daily moves in the sample (5 and 26 October 2026) followed the two rounds of the presidential election; the stress is visible in the volatility of the DI1 futures traded on B3 on those days. A covariance PCA is driven by large moves, so we re-ran it without the 1% of days with the largest moves: PC1’s share goes from 91.5% to 88.7%, and the shapes do not change.</li>
<li><strong>A different setup from the book.</strong> The UK case uses 50 tenors from 6M to 25Y over three years; ours uses 9 tenors from 3M to 10Y over twenty years. The percentages are comparable in spirit, not to the decimal.</li>
<li><strong>Interpolated tenors.</strong> In the early years, with fewer vertices, the long tenors rely on wider interpolation.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section id="next-questions" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="next-questions">Next questions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Can the three factors hedge a DI position out of sample?</strong> Estimate the PCA on a past window, build hedge ratios from the loadings, and measure how well the hedge holds in the following period. Then look for the estimation window that keeps the hedge stable — and test whether a learning approach can improve it.</li>
</ul>
