<div id="quarto-appendix" class="default"><section id="appendix" class="level2 appendix"><h2 class="anchored quarto-appendix-heading">Appendix</h2><div class="quarto-appendix-contents">

<p><strong>PCA, step by step.</strong> Let <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X"> be the <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?T%20%5Ctimes%20n"> matrix of daily changes (<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?T"> days, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?n%20=%209"> tenors), centered, and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?V"> its covariance matrix.</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><strong>Eigen-decomposition.</strong> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?V%20=%20W%20%5CLambda%20W%5E%5Ctop">, with <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?W"> orthogonal (<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?W%5E%7B-1%7D%20=%20W%5E%5Ctop">) and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5CLambda%20=%20%5Ctext%7Bdiag%7D(%5Clambda_1,%20%5Cdots,%20%5Clambda_n)">, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda_1%20%5Cge%20%5Cdots%20%5Cge%20%5Clambda_n%20%5Cge%200">.</li>
<li><strong>Principal components.</strong> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?P%20=%20XW">. Column <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?k"> of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?P"> is the time series of component <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?k"> (its <em>scores</em>). The components are uncorrelated, and the variance of component <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?k"> is <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda_k">.</li>
<li><strong>Representation.</strong> Since <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?W"> is orthogonal, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X%20=%20P%20W%5E%5Ctop">: each tenor’s daily change is a weighted sum of the components, with the loadings as weights, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5CDelta%20r_%7Bi,t%7D%20=%20%5Csum_%7Bk=1%7D%5E%7Bn%7D%20w_%7Bik%7D%5C,%20P_%7Bk,t%7D%20%5Capprox%20%5Csum_%7Bk=1%7D%5E%7B3%7D%20w_%7Bik%7D%5C,%20P_%7Bk,t%7D."> Keeping only the first three columns gives the approximation, whose quality is the share of variance <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csum_%7Bk%20%5Cle%203%7D%20%5Clambda_k%20/%20%5Csum_j%20%5Clambda_j">.</li>
<li><strong>Signs.</strong> Each eigenvector is defined up to its sign (<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?w"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?-w"> are both eigenvectors). We follow the book: PC1 positive, PC2 and PC3 positive at the short end.</li>
<li><strong>Correlation version.</strong> With <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?D"> the diagonal matrix of standard deviations, the correlation matrix is <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?C%20=%20D%5E%7B-1%7D%20V%20D%5E%7B-1%7D">. The PCA of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?C"> is the PCA of the standardized changes; its eigenvectors are not a simple transformation of those of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?V">, which is why the two shapes differ.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Flat-forward interpolation.</strong> B3 publishes the PRE curve at fixed calendar-day vertices and at DI1 contract maturities, not at constant maturities. Each tenor is interpolated between the two surrounding vertices <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t_1%20%3C%20t%20%3C%20t_2"> (in business days) assuming a constant forward rate between them, which means interpolating the log discount factor linearly:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cln%20D(t)%20=%20%5Cln%20D(t_1)%20+%20%5Cfrac%7Bt%20-%20t_1%7D%7Bt_2%20-%20t_1%7D%5C,%5Cbig(%5Cln%20D(t_2)%20-%20%5Cln%20D(t_1)%5Cbig),%20%5Cqquad%20D(t)%20=%20(1%20+%20r_t)%5E%7B-t/252%7D%0A"></p>
<p>Tenors beyond the longest available vertex are left empty rather than extrapolated. The implementation is <code>flat_forward()</code> in <code>build_dataset.py</code>.</p>


